# Spec Platform — Data Model

> The spec IS a knowledge graph. The LLM IS the query engine. Every entity is a node. Every relationship is an edge. Tables encode structured data. The LLM traverses the graph at query time.

---

## Core Ontology

### Entity: Node

A typed node in the spec knowledge graph. Every entity, task, prediction, and concept is a Node.

```yaml
entity: Node
type: entity
properties:
  id:
    type: string
    format: uuid
    required: true
  type:
    type: enum
    values: [project, entity, relationship, event, capability, constraint, screen, flow, user_story, task, failure, edge_case, assumption, prediction, scenario, material, component, file]
    required: true
  name:
    type: string
    required: true
  description:
    type: string
    required: false
  properties:
    type: json
    required: false
states: [created, updated, archived]
lifecycle: created → updated → archived
```

### Entity: Task

A unit of work. Has dependencies, assignment, effort estimates, and a computed completion probability.

```yaml
entity: Task
type: entity
properties:
  id:
    type: string
    format: T##
    required: true
  name:
    type: string
    required: true
  status:
    type: enum
    values: [specified, building, built, verified, shipped]
    required: true
  priority:
    type: enum
    values: [P0, P1, P2, P3]
    required: true
  estimated_hours:
    type: number
    required: false
  actual_hours:
    type: number
    required: false
  depends_on:
    type: string[]
    required: false
  assigned_to:
    type: string
    required: false
  completion_probability:
    type: number
    required: false
    constraints: 0.0-1.0, computed from velocity
states: [specified, building, built, verified, shipped]
lifecycle: specified → building → built → verified → shipped
```

### Entity: Prediction

A forecast about the future state of the project. Has a trigger, timeframe, confidence score, and is scored against actual outcomes.

```yaml
entity: Prediction
type: prediction
properties:
  id:
    type: string
    required: true
  statement:
    type: string
    required: true
  trigger:
    type: string
    required: false
  timeframe:
    type: string
    required: false
  confidence:
    type: number
    required: true
    constraints: 0.0-1.0
  measurement:
    type: string
    required: false
  actual_outcome:
    type: enum
    values: [pending, correct, wrong, partially_correct]
    required: false
states: [pending, resolved]
lifecycle: pending → resolved
```

### Entity: Compile

The compilation process. Reads all .dog files, parses entities and relationships, outputs a .dag graph with integrity hash and token savings report.

```yaml
entity: Compile
type: event
properties:
  source_files:
    type: number
    required: true
  output_hash:
    type: string
    required: true
  token_savings_pct:
    type: number
    required: false
states: [running, completed, failed]
lifecycle: running → completed
```

### Entity: DAG

The compiled graph output. A deterministic JSON structure with typed nodes, edges, integrity metadata, and provable token savings. Queryable by AI agents via MCP.

```yaml
entity: DAG
type: entity
properties:
  version:
    type: string
    required: true
  sha256:
    type: string
    required: true
  source_files:
    type: number
    required: true
  token_savings_pct:
    type: number
    required: false
states: [current, stale]
lifecycle: current → stale
```

### Relationship: Compile → DAG

```yaml
relationship: Compile → DAG
verb: produces
cardinality: 1:1
required: false
cascade: nullify
invariants: DAG is the compiled output of all .dog files
```

---

### Relationship: Node → Node

```
relationship: Node -> Node
  verb: connects
  cardinality: N:M
  required: false
  cascade: none
  invariants: no self-loops. spec graph is a DAG (directed acyclic graph).
  example: SPEC.dog.user_stories → implemented_by → Task[]
```

### Relationship: Task → Task

```
relationship: Task -> Task
  verb: depends_on
  cardinality: N:M
  required: false
  cascade: restrict
  invariants: no cycles. must form a DAG.
  example: T08 ($ button) → depends_on → T04 (SDK). T09 (flow 1) → depends_on → T08.
```

### Relationship: Prediction → Task

```
relationship: Prediction -> Task
  verb: measures
  cardinality: 1:N
  required: false
  cascade: nullify
  invariants: prediction.trigger must be measurable from task completion
  example: Prediction "ships July 15" → measures → Task.completion_probability
```

